# Defect 391, 2026-10-06 (panel 187's R2 mutant `interp-js`, read by the
# coordinator at batch 12's gate). Symptom: JavaScript's template literal
# `` `ключ${x}` `` in a hole of `print(f"...")` was told seven times,
# `unexpected_character` at its two backticks, its four Cyrillic letters and
# its `$`, under `check` and `check --permissive` alike; seven of the eight
# refused characters a file tells, so the next mistake of its kind was the
# last one told. Cause: the scanner refuses a character the syntax does not
# have one at a time (`scan.punct`), and a string written in another
# language's terms is one mistake. A backtick opening a string that closes
# on its line is read to its close as JavaScript reads one and told once,
# by name, with the `f"…"` or `"…"` that writes it as a guess
# (`backtick_strings.hero`); the runs are the case beside this one. A
# backtick this language reads as a stray one stays one message: its close
# in a string, or a comment begun after it. A template written over two
# lines is not one stretch: the lexer reads a line at a time.

function in_a_hole(x: i64)
    m: {str: i64} @= {}
    m[f"ключ{x}"] @ 2
    print(f"{m[`ключ${x}`].must()}")  #~ unexpected_character

function outside(x: i64)
    print(`v=${x}`)  #~ unexpected_character

function no_hole()
    print(`say "hi"`)  #~ unexpected_character

function nested(x: i64)
    print(`a${`b${x}`}c`)  #~ unexpected_character

# A `}` in the template closed the hole it stands in, a `lone_brace` after.
function brace_in_a_hole()
    print(f"{`a}b`}")  #~ unexpected_character

function stray_before_a_comment()
    #~v unexpected_character
    x = 1 ` # the `x` above
    print(x)

function closed_in_a_string()
    x = ` "a`b"  #~ unexpected_character
    print(x)

function main()
    in_a_hole(7)
    outside(7)
    no_hole()
    nested(7)
    brace_in_a_hole()
    stray_before_a_comment()
    closed_in_a_string()

# Corrected 2026-10-06, defect 407 (lane b13-tmpl407, `03779be8`), written
# below the program so no line above moves: the header's last sentence, *a
# template written over two lines is not one stretch*, is false from that
# repair on. A template written over lines is read to its close, as a block
# comment is, and told once where its lines could not be code
# (`backtick_strings.template_end`); see
# `fixedbugs-407-a-template-over-lines-is-told-once`.
